1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,699
A twenty-three-hundred-dollar bill took the home

2
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:03,759
this woman spent years paying off —

3
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:05,451
and every cent was legal.

4
00:00:08,355 --> 00:00:11,535
A true story about a small debt, a locked door,

5
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:13,874
and a government that took far more

6
00:00:13,875 --> 00:00:14,934
than it was owed —

7
00:00:15,455 --> 00:00:17,574
and about a right you may not know you have,

8
00:00:17,575 --> 00:00:20,654
until the day one late tax check could cost you

9
00:00:20,655 --> 00:00:21,455
yours.

10
00:00:23,951 --> 00:00:24,650
For years,

11
00:00:24,651 --> 00:00:27,230
this one-bedroom condo had been Geraldine's whole

12
00:00:27,231 --> 00:00:28,031
world —

13
00:00:28,371 --> 00:00:29,900
the home she had paid off,

14
00:00:30,131 --> 00:00:31,350
where the mornings once came

15
00:00:31,351 --> 00:00:32,704
in through that window.

16
00:00:33,421 --> 00:00:35,960
In her eighties, worried about living alone,

17
00:00:35,961 --> 00:00:37,560
she moved to a senior community

18
00:00:37,561 --> 00:00:38,679
for her own safety.

19
00:00:39,061 --> 00:00:40,532
She never sold the condo.

20
00:00:41,126 --> 00:00:43,008
She kept it, empty and paid for,

21
00:00:43,406 --> 00:00:44,865
the way you keep a place still full

22
00:00:44,866 --> 00:00:45,666
of your life.

23
00:00:46,463 --> 00:00:48,842
The furniture stayed where she had left it —

24
00:00:48,843 --> 00:00:50,022
the kitchen that had held

25
00:00:50,023 --> 00:00:51,670
a thousand ordinary dinners,

26
00:00:52,163 --> 00:00:53,242
the window that had framed

27
00:00:53,243 --> 00:00:56,067
a thousand ordinary mornings, all of it waiting,

28
00:00:56,563 --> 00:00:58,269
the way a life waits when you

29
00:00:58,859 --> 00:01:00,800
are not quite ready to let it go.

30
00:01:01,549 --> 00:01:03,255
Her name was Geraldine Tyler,

31
00:01:03,409 --> 00:01:05,548
and by the time this story finds its ending,

32
00:01:05,549 --> 00:01:07,255
she is ninety-four years old.

33
00:01:07,908 --> 00:01:09,907
She had bought that little place in Minneapolis

34
00:01:09,908 --> 00:01:12,487
and made it hers one payment at a time,

35
00:01:12,488 --> 00:01:14,247
over years of ordinary work,

36
00:01:14,248 --> 00:01:17,168
until the loan was gone and the deed was clean.

37
00:01:18,214 --> 00:01:19,773
Owning it outright was supposed

38
00:01:19,774 --> 00:01:21,009
to be the safe part —

39
00:01:21,534 --> 00:01:24,005
the one piece of a long life that no bank,

40
00:01:24,294 --> 00:01:26,654
no landlord, no downturn could reach.

41
00:01:27,684 --> 00:01:30,003
But a home you no longer sleep in still owes

42
00:01:30,004 --> 00:01:31,763
the city something every year.

43
00:01:31,764 --> 00:01:32,564
Property tax.

44
00:01:33,485 --> 00:01:35,084
And once Geraldine moved out,

45
00:01:35,085 --> 00:01:37,846
the bills kept arriving at a door no one opened.

46
00:01:37,847 --> 00:01:39,318
Her tax had gone unpaid —

47
00:01:39,867 --> 00:01:40,691
not a fortune,

48
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:43,085
not a mansion's worth of back taxes.

49
00:01:43,741 --> 00:01:46,202
About two thousand three hundred dollars.

50
00:01:46,802 --> 00:01:48,214
The price of a used car.

51
00:01:48,888 --> 00:01:49,667
One missed line,

52
00:01:49,668 --> 00:01:52,256
sitting quietly in a county ledger, waiting.

53
00:01:52,878 --> 00:01:55,237
And this is where the machine begins to hum —

54
00:01:55,238 --> 00:01:57,637
because an unpaid tax bill does not simply sit

55
00:01:57,638 --> 00:01:58,438
and wait.

56
00:01:58,558 --> 00:01:59,358
It grows.

57
00:01:59,738 --> 00:02:01,326
The county added a penalty.

58
00:02:01,498 --> 00:02:02,322
Then interest.

59
00:02:03,091 --> 00:02:05,973
Then costs, and fees layered on top of the costs,

60
00:02:06,491 --> 00:02:08,903
month after month, quarter after quarter,

61
00:02:09,071 --> 00:02:11,130
with no hand anywhere on the brake.

62
00:02:11,772 --> 00:02:14,007
So here is where it begins to tighten.

63
00:02:14,322 --> 00:02:17,087
By the time anyone read the figure back to her,

64
00:02:17,142 --> 00:02:19,301
twenty-three hundred dollars had swollen

65
00:02:19,302 --> 00:02:21,023
to roughly fifteen thousand.

66
00:02:21,563 --> 00:02:24,504
The debt had multiplied more than six times over —

67
00:02:24,703 --> 00:02:27,409
not because she borrowed a single dollar more,

68
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:30,136
not because she gambled or spent or ran,

69
00:02:30,603 --> 00:02:32,422
but because the clock kept running

70
00:02:32,423 --> 00:02:33,903
and the law let it run.

71
00:02:34,934 --> 00:02:37,405
Think about what that number really means.

72
00:02:37,717 --> 00:02:41,196
Not fifteen thousand dollars of luxury, or fraud,

73
00:02:41,197 --> 00:02:42,491
or anything she chose.

74
00:02:42,868 --> 00:02:45,447
Fifteen thousand dollars of paperwork,

75
00:02:45,448 --> 00:02:48,007
growing on its own against a woman in her nineties

76
00:02:48,008 --> 00:02:49,927
who had already paid off the very home it

77
00:02:49,928 --> 00:02:51,457
was now quietly consuming.

78
00:02:52,059 --> 00:02:53,658
She had done the hard thing.

79
00:02:53,659 --> 00:02:54,659
She had finished.

80
00:02:55,445 --> 00:02:57,504
And the finishing did not save her.

81
00:02:57,903 --> 00:03:00,432
This was not a loophole aimed at her alone;

82
00:03:00,503 --> 00:03:02,782
if your own property-tax bill had ever slipped

83
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:03,842
past its due date,

84
00:03:04,283 --> 00:03:05,922
the same process could have taken

85
00:03:05,923 --> 00:03:07,158
the equity you built.

86
00:03:07,791 --> 00:03:09,310
So sit with the question that runs

87
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:10,664
under this whole story:

88
00:03:11,071 --> 00:03:12,690
when a system like that starts to move

89
00:03:12,691 --> 00:03:15,515
against one ordinary person over one small debt,

90
00:03:16,031 --> 00:03:18,913
is there anything left that can actually stop it?

91
00:03:19,271 --> 00:03:20,071
Not slow it.

92
00:03:20,691 --> 00:03:21,491
Stop it.

93
00:03:22,137 --> 00:03:24,556
And by the time anyone added it up, the paperwork

94
00:03:24,557 --> 00:03:26,656
to sell her home was already moving —

95
00:03:26,657 --> 00:03:29,017
across town, in a county office,

96
00:03:29,637 --> 00:03:31,256
a file with her address on it,

97
00:03:31,257 --> 00:03:32,551
sliding toward a sale.

98
00:03:35,132 --> 00:03:37,371
The county did not send a lawyer to her door.

99
00:03:37,372 --> 00:03:38,490
It did not have to.

100
00:03:39,261 --> 00:03:41,860
Because under the state's tax-forfeiture law,

101
00:03:41,861 --> 00:03:43,980
there was no trial to win and no judge

102
00:03:43,981 --> 00:03:44,781
to persuade.

103
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:47,561
The process ran on its own rails.

104
00:03:48,356 --> 00:03:49,532
Title to the condo —

105
00:03:49,656 --> 00:03:50,950
the ownership itself —

106
00:03:51,116 --> 00:03:52,695
passed out of Geraldine's hands

107
00:03:52,696 --> 00:03:53,990
and into the county's.

108
00:03:54,529 --> 00:03:54,868
And then

109
00:03:54,869 --> 00:03:56,908
the county did what any creditor does when it

110
00:03:56,909 --> 00:03:57,909
holds a property:

111
00:03:58,189 --> 00:03:59,895
it put the place up for sale.

112
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:02,900
It sold for forty thousand dollars.

113
00:04:03,438 --> 00:04:05,673
Now hold that number against the debt.

114
00:04:06,267 --> 00:04:08,267
She owed about fifteen thousand —

115
00:04:08,487 --> 00:04:11,193
the original tax, the penalties, the interest,

116
00:04:11,227 --> 00:04:12,580
the fees, all of it in.

117
00:04:13,554 --> 00:04:15,613
The sale brought in forty thousand.

118
00:04:16,197 --> 00:04:17,516
Any bank foreclosing

119
00:04:17,517 --> 00:04:19,956
on any house takes what it is owed and hands

120
00:04:19,957 --> 00:04:21,545
the rest back to the owner.

121
00:04:21,905 --> 00:04:23,304
That is what makes it a debt

122
00:04:23,305 --> 00:04:24,364
and not a seizure.

123
00:04:25,106 --> 00:04:27,226
But that is not what happened here.

124
00:04:28,167 --> 00:04:30,206
The county kept all forty thousand.

125
00:04:30,207 --> 00:04:31,325
Every dollar of it.

126
00:04:31,971 --> 00:04:34,736
It took the fifteen thousand she actually owed,

127
00:04:34,971 --> 00:04:36,850
and then it kept the twenty-five thousand

128
00:04:36,851 --> 00:04:37,733
that was hers —

129
00:04:38,171 --> 00:04:39,951
the surplus, the equity,

130
00:04:40,511 --> 00:04:42,670
the paid-off value of a home she had earned across

131
00:04:42,671 --> 00:04:43,789
a lifetime of work.

132
00:04:44,692 --> 00:04:46,851
And that surplus did not go into some account

133
00:04:46,852 --> 00:04:49,381
with her name on it, waiting to be claimed.

134
00:04:49,890 --> 00:04:51,949
Under the law, it was simply gone —

135
00:04:52,210 --> 00:04:54,151
split among the county, the town,

136
00:04:54,250 --> 00:04:55,721
and the school district —

137
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:58,106
and there was no form she could file,

138
00:04:58,190 --> 00:04:59,661
no office she could call,

139
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:01,549
no way to ask for a single cent

140
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:02,350
of it back.

141
00:05:03,075 --> 00:05:04,654
The door was not just locked.

142
00:05:04,655 --> 00:05:05,714
There was no door.

143
00:05:06,276 --> 00:05:08,415
So Geraldine did the only thing left to do.

144
00:05:08,416 --> 00:05:09,216
She sued.

145
00:05:09,895 --> 00:05:10,494
She argued

146
00:05:10,495 --> 00:05:12,354
that taking twenty-five thousand dollars

147
00:05:12,355 --> 00:05:13,594
of her property to settle

148
00:05:13,595 --> 00:05:16,094
a fifteen-thousand-dollar debt was a taking

149
00:05:16,095 --> 00:05:17,634
under the Fifth Amendment —

150
00:05:17,635 --> 00:05:19,454
the government seizing private property without

151
00:05:19,455 --> 00:05:20,337
paying for it —

152
00:05:20,715 --> 00:05:22,375
and, in effect, a fine

153
00:05:23,405 --> 00:05:26,124
so far beyond the offense that it also violated

154
00:05:26,125 --> 00:05:28,831
the Eighth Amendment's ban on excessive fines.

155
00:05:28,974 --> 00:05:30,753
And notice what she was asking for.

156
00:05:30,754 --> 00:05:31,554
Not the home.

157
00:05:32,453 --> 00:05:34,277
Not even a fight over the tax —

158
00:05:34,413 --> 00:05:35,884
she agreed she owed that.

159
00:05:36,304 --> 00:05:37,539
Only the change back:

160
00:05:37,824 --> 00:05:39,423
the difference between the small debt

161
00:05:39,424 --> 00:05:42,306
and the far larger value the county had pocketed.

162
00:05:42,988 --> 00:05:44,387
A woman near the end of her life,

163
00:05:44,388 --> 00:05:46,948
asking for what was already, plainly, hers.

164
00:05:48,093 --> 00:05:49,612
The same overdue-tax rule

165
00:05:49,613 --> 00:05:51,812
that had just swallowed her equity was still

166
00:05:51,813 --> 00:05:53,332
on the books in your state,

167
00:05:53,333 --> 00:05:55,152
waiting for one late check of yours —

168
00:05:55,153 --> 00:05:56,553
until this ruling.

169
00:05:57,563 --> 00:06:00,210
And at first, the law did not agree with her.

170
00:06:00,531 --> 00:06:02,450
The federal district court dismissed her case

171
00:06:02,451 --> 00:06:03,251
in 2020.

172
00:06:04,289 --> 00:06:05,089
She appealed,

173
00:06:05,389 --> 00:06:07,988
and in 2022 the federal court of appeals affirmed

174
00:06:07,989 --> 00:06:08,930
that dismissal —

175
00:06:09,389 --> 00:06:11,565
the same answer, one level higher up.

176
00:06:12,089 --> 00:06:12,889
Two courts.

177
00:06:13,209 --> 00:06:14,009
Two losses.

178
00:06:14,734 --> 00:06:16,969
She had asked for the difference back.

179
00:06:17,192 --> 00:06:18,791
The answer was a single word:

180
00:06:18,792 --> 00:06:19,592
nothing.

181
00:06:20,021 --> 00:06:22,360
And when she went to court, the court agreed —

182
00:06:22,361 --> 00:06:23,581
not once, but twice.

183
00:06:24,708 --> 00:06:26,427
Which left exactly one door

184
00:06:26,428 --> 00:06:27,847
in the country still unopened,

185
00:06:27,848 --> 00:06:29,789
at the top of a marble staircase,

186
00:06:30,128 --> 00:06:32,069
and one question hanging over it:

187
00:06:32,668 --> 00:06:35,047
could nine justices in Washington look at all

188
00:06:35,048 --> 00:06:36,872
this and finally call it wrong?

189
00:06:39,468 --> 00:06:41,292
Geraldine was not the only one.

190
00:06:42,065 --> 00:06:43,065
In another state,

191
00:06:43,425 --> 00:06:44,884
another homeowner had already carried

192
00:06:44,885 --> 00:06:47,024
this same fight into a federal court —

193
00:06:47,025 --> 00:06:48,164
and won a first round

194
00:06:48,165 --> 00:06:50,577
the county had assumed it could not lose.

195
00:06:51,164 --> 00:06:53,243
The legal foundation representing Geraldine

196
00:06:53,244 --> 00:06:55,703
had been fighting cases like hers all across

197
00:06:55,704 --> 00:06:58,564
the country, in state after state,

198
00:06:59,144 --> 00:07:00,673
homeowner after homeowner.

199
00:07:01,470 --> 00:07:01,829
And one

200
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:04,771
of those cases looked almost exactly like her own.

201
00:07:05,274 --> 00:07:06,980
The same kind of small debt —

202
00:07:07,254 --> 00:07:09,489
a fraction of what the home was worth.

203
00:07:09,634 --> 00:07:11,281
The same disappearing house.

204
00:07:12,004 --> 00:07:14,423
And the same government keeping everything above

205
00:07:14,424 --> 00:07:15,424
what it was owed.

206
00:07:16,366 --> 00:07:18,205
But look at what was done with that home once

207
00:07:18,206 --> 00:07:19,324
the county held it.

208
00:07:19,985 --> 00:07:21,985
It was not simply sold at auction.

209
00:07:22,814 --> 00:07:24,493
It was handed to a private entity

210
00:07:24,494 --> 00:07:25,729
for a single dollar —

211
00:07:26,374 --> 00:07:27,174
one dollar —

212
00:07:27,534 --> 00:07:29,299
and later resold for far more.

213
00:07:30,241 --> 00:07:31,640
The county and the parties that took

214
00:07:31,641 --> 00:07:34,180
the property kept everything above the small debt

215
00:07:34,181 --> 00:07:35,760
that was owed, exactly

216
00:07:35,761 --> 00:07:38,240
the way Minnesota had kept everything above

217
00:07:38,241 --> 00:07:39,041
Geraldine's.

218
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:41,589
That homeowner fought it —

219
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:43,029
and that case went a different way

220
00:07:43,030 --> 00:07:44,265
than Geraldine's had.

221
00:07:44,862 --> 00:07:45,802
In 2022,

222
00:07:46,162 --> 00:07:48,881
a federal appeals court ruled for the homeowner,

223
00:07:48,882 --> 00:07:50,941
holding that stripping the equity out of a home

224
00:07:50,942 --> 00:07:52,141
and keeping more

225
00:07:52,142 --> 00:07:53,721
than the debt is not a clever use

226
00:07:53,722 --> 00:07:56,487
of the law but a violation of the Constitution.

227
00:07:57,443 --> 00:07:59,002
For the first time, a court

228
00:07:59,003 --> 00:08:01,122
at that level had said out loud what Geraldine

229
00:08:01,123 --> 00:08:02,652
had been saying all along,

230
00:08:02,863 --> 00:08:04,275
and losing on all along.

231
00:08:05,195 --> 00:08:07,430
So look at your own state on this map,

232
00:08:07,575 --> 00:08:09,434
because until very recently the answer

233
00:08:09,435 --> 00:08:10,634
to "could it happen here?"

234
00:08:10,635 --> 00:08:11,675
was yes.

235
00:08:12,807 --> 00:08:15,631
Because this was not a handful of unlucky cases.

236
00:08:16,286 --> 00:08:18,168
By one legal foundation's count,

237
00:08:18,666 --> 00:08:21,025
across the country in less than a decade,

238
00:08:21,026 --> 00:08:23,265
thousands of families had lost their homes exactly

239
00:08:23,266 --> 00:08:24,066
this way —

240
00:08:24,486 --> 00:08:26,192
not just the taxes they owed,

241
00:08:26,506 --> 00:08:29,212
but everything they had built above it, adding

242
00:08:29,564 --> 00:08:31,663
up to hundreds of millions of dollars in equity

243
00:08:31,664 --> 00:08:33,958
that vanished into government accounts.

244
00:08:34,669 --> 00:08:37,081
And Minnesota was among the worst of it —

245
00:08:37,209 --> 00:08:39,888
home after home taken over a debt that was often

246
00:08:39,889 --> 00:08:42,536
a thin sliver of what the property was worth.

247
00:08:42,769 --> 00:08:44,416
A few thousand dollars owed.

248
00:08:44,469 --> 00:08:45,587
A whole house gone.

249
00:08:46,415 --> 00:08:48,944
Multiply Geraldine by a thousand neighbors,

250
00:08:49,355 --> 00:08:50,994
and you start to see the size

251
00:08:50,995 --> 00:08:53,034
of the thing her small case was about to run

252
00:08:53,035 --> 00:08:53,835
into.

253
00:08:54,399 --> 00:08:56,458
And these were not reckless people.

254
00:08:57,089 --> 00:08:59,618
They were the old, the sick, the grieving —

255
00:09:00,189 --> 00:09:02,068
people who fell behind in a hard year

256
00:09:02,069 --> 00:09:03,528
and never once imagined

257
00:09:03,529 --> 00:09:05,308
that a small debt could cost them

258
00:09:05,309 --> 00:09:07,015
the largest thing they owned.

259
00:09:07,581 --> 00:09:09,699
Every dot on that map was a kitchen,

260
00:09:10,201 --> 00:09:11,848
a mortgage finally paid off,

261
00:09:12,181 --> 00:09:14,416
a working life poured into four walls.

262
00:09:15,147 --> 00:09:15,766
And every one

263
00:09:15,767 --> 00:09:17,846
of them had been emptied out over a fraction

264
00:09:17,847 --> 00:09:19,435
of what the home was worth.

265
00:09:19,973 --> 00:09:22,385
But that win came from one appeals court,

266
00:09:22,393 --> 00:09:24,687
binding just one corner of the country.

267
00:09:25,113 --> 00:09:26,525
It proved she was right.

268
00:09:27,121 --> 00:09:29,180
It did not yet protect anyone else.

269
00:09:29,811 --> 00:09:31,950
Only the justices in Washington could make

270
00:09:31,951 --> 00:09:33,716
that rule the law everywhere —

271
00:09:34,151 --> 00:09:36,030
and Geraldine's case was the one climbing

272
00:09:36,031 --> 00:09:36,831
that far.

273
00:09:39,509 --> 00:09:40,528
The county walked

274
00:09:40,529 --> 00:09:42,470
into the Supreme Court confident.

275
00:09:43,081 --> 00:09:44,434
And to be fair to them,

276
00:09:44,721 --> 00:09:46,133
they had a reason to be.

277
00:09:46,700 --> 00:09:48,399
Their argument was not lawless.

278
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:49,576
It was the opposite.

279
00:09:50,226 --> 00:09:52,814
They had followed the statute to the letter.

280
00:09:53,148 --> 00:09:54,627
The state legislature had written

281
00:09:54,628 --> 00:09:55,746
the forfeiture law;

282
00:09:55,968 --> 00:09:58,247
the county had simply done what the law told it

283
00:09:58,248 --> 00:10:00,542
to do, the way it had done for decades,

284
00:10:01,088 --> 00:10:03,912
the way many places across the country had done.

285
00:10:04,336 --> 00:10:06,630
Nobody, they said, had broken any rule.

286
00:10:06,979 --> 00:10:08,567
The state had passed a law.

287
00:10:09,204 --> 00:10:11,083
The law said the surplus stayed.

288
00:10:11,084 --> 00:10:11,884
Case closed.

289
00:10:12,683 --> 00:10:13,982
And if you had ever been late

290
00:10:13,983 --> 00:10:15,748
on a single property-tax bill,

291
00:10:16,263 --> 00:10:18,202
this same surplus-seizure machine

292
00:10:18,203 --> 00:10:20,203
could have reached your house too.

293
00:10:20,806 --> 00:10:23,512
So the whole case sharpened to a single point.

294
00:10:24,192 --> 00:10:26,486
If a legislature simply writes it down,

295
00:10:26,772 --> 00:10:28,311
can the government keep the money left

296
00:10:28,312 --> 00:10:29,892
over after a debt is paid?

297
00:10:30,922 --> 00:10:32,040
And to answer that,

298
00:10:32,082 --> 00:10:33,461
Geraldine's lawyers did something

299
00:10:33,462 --> 00:10:34,991
the county did not expect.

300
00:10:35,702 --> 00:10:37,281
They stopped arguing about Minnesota —

301
00:10:37,282 --> 00:10:40,161
and started arguing about eight hundred years

302
00:10:40,162 --> 00:10:40,962
of law.

303
00:10:41,364 --> 00:10:42,343
Because this idea —

304
00:10:42,344 --> 00:10:45,243
that when the state seizes property to settle

305
00:10:45,244 --> 00:10:48,463
a debt, whatever is left over belongs to the owner

306
00:10:48,464 --> 00:10:49,758
and not to the crown —

307
00:10:50,224 --> 00:10:51,823
is one of the oldest promises

308
00:10:51,824 --> 00:10:54,589
in the entire English-speaking legal tradition.

309
00:10:55,106 --> 00:10:57,946
It runs straight back to Magna Carta, in 1215,

310
00:10:58,526 --> 00:11:00,805
which provided
that when the king's officers seized

311
00:11:00,806 --> 00:11:03,405
a debtor's goods, the residue was to be left

312
00:11:03,406 --> 00:11:05,288
to carry out that person's will.

313
00:11:05,923 --> 00:11:08,335
The surplus was never the king's to keep.

314
00:11:08,799 --> 00:11:09,058
Even

315
00:11:09,059 --> 00:11:11,178
a medieval crown had to give back what was left

316
00:11:11,179 --> 00:11:11,979
over.

317
00:11:12,418 --> 00:11:13,937
And the principle did not stop

318
00:11:13,938 --> 00:11:15,467
in the thirteenth century.

319
00:11:16,083 --> 00:11:19,603
By 1692, English statute spelled it out in plain,

320
00:11:20,023 --> 00:11:20,822
hard words —

321
00:11:20,823 --> 00:11:22,902
that any overplus from such

322
00:11:22,903 --> 00:11:25,197
a sale had to be restored to the owner.

323
00:11:25,363 --> 00:11:26,775
Not returned as a mercy.

324
00:11:27,643 --> 00:11:29,584
Not returned if you asked nicely.

325
00:11:29,823 --> 00:11:30,823
Required, by law.

326
00:11:31,726 --> 00:11:33,125
The rule the county was defending

327
00:11:33,126 --> 00:11:35,538
in the twenty-first century had, in fact,

328
00:11:36,066 --> 00:11:38,525
been the forbidden exception to a much older rule

329
00:11:38,526 --> 00:11:40,879
for the better part of a thousand years.

330
00:11:41,150 --> 00:11:43,915
Picture the distance that promise had traveled.

331
00:11:44,304 --> 00:11:46,903
From barons forcing a king's hand in a meadow

332
00:11:46,904 --> 00:11:49,845
at Runnymede, through centuries of English courts,

333
00:11:50,044 --> 00:11:52,823
across an ocean, into the founding of a country

334
00:11:52,824 --> 00:11:55,123
that wrote property protection straight

335
00:11:55,124 --> 00:11:56,477
into its Constitution —

336
00:11:57,124 --> 00:11:58,477
and then down, finally,

337
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:02,006
into a routine county tax office in Minnesota,

338
00:12:02,140 --> 00:12:04,259
where somewhere along the way the oldest part

339
00:12:04,260 --> 00:12:06,554
of the promise had simply been dropped.

340
00:12:07,005 --> 00:12:08,864
The county had kept the machinery.

341
00:12:08,865 --> 00:12:10,453
It had forgotten the limit.

342
00:12:11,042 --> 00:12:13,160
And it was not only ancient history.

343
00:12:13,918 --> 00:12:16,800
The Supreme Court's own past said the same thing.

344
00:12:17,026 --> 00:12:18,205
The Court had recognized

345
00:12:18,206 --> 00:12:19,725
this surplus-return principle

346
00:12:19,726 --> 00:12:20,685
in its own decisions —

347
00:12:20,686 --> 00:12:24,286
as far back as 1884, and again in 1980.

348
00:12:25,381 --> 00:12:27,100
So this was not some novel theory

349
00:12:27,101 --> 00:12:28,520
a ninety-four-year-old had invented

350
00:12:28,521 --> 00:12:29,580
to save her condo.

351
00:12:30,393 --> 00:12:32,532
It was a rule woven through centuries of law

352
00:12:32,533 --> 00:12:34,772
that the county's everyday practice had quietly

353
00:12:34,773 --> 00:12:35,773
walked away from.

354
00:12:36,519 --> 00:12:38,813
Which is why, when Geraldine's lawyer —

355
00:12:39,239 --> 00:12:41,438
Christina Martin, of the legal foundation

356
00:12:41,439 --> 00:12:43,027
that had carried her case —

357
00:12:43,579 --> 00:12:46,298
stood before the justices on April twenty-sixth,

358
00:12:46,299 --> 00:12:47,099
2023,

359
00:12:47,859 --> 00:12:50,478
the county's confident argument had a very deep

360
00:12:50,479 --> 00:12:51,279
problem.

361
00:12:51,887 --> 00:12:53,475
It rested on a single idea:

362
00:12:53,927 --> 00:12:54,686
that writing a rule

363
00:12:54,687 --> 00:12:57,566
into a modern statute could quietly erase a right

364
00:12:57,567 --> 00:12:58,367
this old.

365
00:12:59,081 --> 00:13:01,140
And eight centuries of law were now stacked

366
00:13:01,141 --> 00:13:02,906
on the other side of the room.

367
00:13:03,118 --> 00:13:04,824
It sounded almost reasonable.

368
00:13:05,297 --> 00:13:06,216
Until you realize

369
00:13:06,217 --> 00:13:08,996
the answer was written down eight hundred years

370
00:13:08,997 --> 00:13:10,821
ago, on a riverbank in England.

371
00:13:13,323 --> 00:13:15,963
In 1215, a cornered king sealed a promise:

372
00:13:16,663 --> 00:13:19,002
when the crown seized a dead man's goods to settle

373
00:13:19,003 --> 00:13:21,602
a debt, whatever was left over had to go back

374
00:13:21,603 --> 00:13:22,427
to his family.

375
00:13:23,211 --> 00:13:25,917
The residue belonged to them, not to the king.

376
00:13:26,690 --> 00:13:28,049
That principle outlived him

377
00:13:28,050 --> 00:13:29,497
by eight hundred years.

378
00:13:30,123 --> 00:13:32,382
The rule those nine justices were about to touch

379
00:13:32,383 --> 00:13:34,342
is the same rule that could have emptied

380
00:13:34,343 --> 00:13:35,990
the equity in your own home.

381
00:13:36,528 --> 00:13:37,567
Inside the courtroom,

382
00:13:37,568 --> 00:13:39,787
the county's lawyers made their stand:

383
00:13:39,788 --> 00:13:42,528
the statute was clear, title had passed,

384
00:13:43,008 --> 00:13:45,008
the surplus belonged to the state.

385
00:13:45,673 --> 00:13:47,913
And the justices pressed back, hard,

386
00:13:48,313 --> 00:13:51,137
with the question that undoes the whole scheme —

387
00:13:51,473 --> 00:13:54,212
if the government can keep twenty-five thousand

388
00:13:54,213 --> 00:13:56,801
dollars over a fifteen-thousand-dollar debt,

389
00:13:57,273 --> 00:13:58,352
then what, exactly,

390
00:13:58,353 --> 00:14:00,432
stops it from keeping an entire house

391
00:14:00,433 --> 00:14:01,962
over a hundred-dollar one?

392
00:14:02,153 --> 00:14:03,212
Where is the line?

393
00:14:03,353 --> 00:14:04,412
Where does it end?

394
00:14:05,035 --> 00:14:07,976
It was the kind of question a rule cannot survive.

395
00:14:08,654 --> 00:14:09,993
Because the moment you admit

396
00:14:09,994 --> 00:14:11,693
the government has to stop somewhere

397
00:14:11,694 --> 00:14:14,518
between a hundred-dollar debt and a whole house,

398
00:14:14,734 --> 00:14:15,713
you have already admitted

399
00:14:15,714 --> 00:14:17,832
the surplus was never really theirs.

400
00:14:18,681 --> 00:14:21,152
You are no longer arguing about principle.

401
00:14:21,649 --> 00:14:23,508
You are only haggling over how much

402
00:14:23,509 --> 00:14:26,274
of someone else's money the state gets to keep.

403
00:14:26,614 --> 00:14:28,433
There was no good answer to that question,

404
00:14:28,434 --> 00:14:29,963
because there was no line.

405
00:14:30,836 --> 00:14:32,816
And on May twenty-fifth, 2023,

406
00:14:33,036 --> 00:14:34,595
the Court gave its own answer —

407
00:14:34,596 --> 00:14:35,856
and it was not close.

408
00:14:36,916 --> 00:14:38,622
By a vote of nine to nothing,

409
00:14:38,736 --> 00:14:40,915
the Supreme Court ruled for Geraldine Tyler,

410
00:14:40,916 --> 00:14:42,395
in an opinion written

411
00:14:42,396 --> 00:14:44,161
by Chief Justice John Roberts.

412
00:14:44,946 --> 00:14:46,765
Nine justices who agree

413
00:14:46,766 --> 00:14:49,354
on almost nothing agreed completely on this.

414
00:14:49,958 --> 00:14:51,487
And the holding was exact.

415
00:14:52,137 --> 00:14:54,216
The government can collect the tax you owe.

416
00:14:54,217 --> 00:14:55,629
It cannot keep the rest.

417
00:14:56,266 --> 00:14:58,205
Taking the surplus value above the debt,

418
00:14:58,206 --> 00:15:00,985
the Court said, is a taking of private property

419
00:15:00,986 --> 00:15:02,574
under the Fifth Amendment —

420
00:15:02,746 --> 00:15:05,245
and the Constitution requires that you be paid

421
00:15:05,246 --> 00:15:06,305
for what is taken.

422
00:15:06,943 --> 00:15:08,642
The Court did not even need to reach

423
00:15:08,643 --> 00:15:10,582
the separate excessive-fines question to say

424
00:15:10,583 --> 00:15:11,383
that much;

425
00:15:11,683 --> 00:15:13,330
the taking alone decided it.

426
00:15:13,952 --> 00:15:16,128
But two of the justices went further.

427
00:15:16,735 --> 00:15:19,295
In a concurring opinion, Justice Gorsuch,

428
00:15:19,795 --> 00:15:21,335
joined by Justice Jackson,

429
00:15:21,915 --> 00:15:24,091
warned that a penalty like this one —

430
00:15:24,255 --> 00:15:27,137
a rule built to punish people and to deter them —

431
00:15:27,195 --> 00:15:29,935
is really a fine by another name, the very

432
00:15:30,849 --> 00:15:32,788
thing the Eighth Amendment was written to keep

433
00:15:32,789 --> 00:15:33,589
in check.

434
00:15:34,235 --> 00:15:36,823
The majority left that door for another day.

435
00:15:37,157 --> 00:15:39,716
But those two justices flagged it, in writing,

436
00:15:39,717 --> 00:15:42,070
for the next case that walks through it.

437
00:15:42,819 --> 00:15:44,599
And the case itself —

438
00:15:44,879 --> 00:15:46,898
the one the whole country now points

439
00:15:46,899 --> 00:15:48,858
to when this happens —

440
00:15:48,859 --> 00:15:51,739
has a fixed address in the law:

441
00:15:51,939 --> 00:15:54,650
five ninety-eight U.S. six thirty-one,

442
00:15:54,651 --> 00:15:55,539
decided in 2023.

443
00:15:55,540 --> 00:15:57,759
Say it plainly so no one gets it wrong.

444
00:15:57,760 --> 00:15:59,560
Not six hundred.

445
00:15:59,706 --> 00:16:00,597
Five ninety-eight.

446
00:16:00,598 --> 00:16:02,797
The rule of the case is simple enough to carry

447
00:16:02,798 --> 00:16:03,598
in one hand:

448
00:16:04,038 --> 00:16:05,817
the state takes what it is owed,

449
00:16:05,818 --> 00:16:07,418
and not one dollar more.

450
00:16:08,443 --> 00:16:10,782
Chief Justice Roberts folded the entire principle

451
00:16:10,783 --> 00:16:11,901
into a single line,

452
00:16:12,523 --> 00:16:14,464
one worth keeping in your pocket.

453
00:16:14,894 --> 00:16:16,294
The taxpayer, he wrote,

454
00:16:16,694 --> 00:16:18,873
must render unto Caesar what is Caesar's,

455
00:16:18,874 --> 00:16:19,674
but no more.

456
00:16:20,074 --> 00:16:21,368
What you owe, you owe.

457
00:16:22,321 --> 00:16:24,340
What is left over the government has no claim

458
00:16:24,341 --> 00:16:25,141
to at all.

459
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:27,139
And here is the part that turns

460
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:29,434
this from one woman's story into yours.

461
00:16:30,022 --> 00:16:32,101
By the time the Court ruled, most states

462
00:16:32,102 --> 00:16:33,941
and the federal government already required

463
00:16:33,942 --> 00:16:35,530
the surplus to be returned.

464
00:16:36,148 --> 00:16:38,007
Geraldine's Minnesota was on the wrong side

465
00:16:38,008 --> 00:16:38,927
of a rule that most

466
00:16:38,928 --> 00:16:40,507
of the country had already accepted

467
00:16:40,508 --> 00:16:41,567
as basic fairness.

468
00:16:42,414 --> 00:16:44,593
She had not been protected by a strange law

469
00:16:44,594 --> 00:16:45,770
in a faraway place —

470
00:16:46,134 --> 00:16:48,053
she had been unprotected by an outlier,

471
00:16:48,054 --> 00:16:50,230
in a country that mostly knew better.

472
00:16:50,909 --> 00:16:52,288
And in the wake of the ruling,

473
00:16:52,289 --> 00:16:54,368
states that had still allowed it began rewriting

474
00:16:54,369 --> 00:16:55,169
these laws —

475
00:16:55,549 --> 00:16:58,249
so that the answer to "could it happen here?"

476
00:16:58,809 --> 00:17:00,750
would finally, everywhere, be no.

477
00:17:01,633 --> 00:17:02,652
The line the Court drew

478
00:17:02,653 --> 00:17:05,594
in Geraldine's name now runs across the whole map,

479
00:17:05,673 --> 00:17:08,548
including the state you are sitting in right now.

480
00:17:08,549 --> 00:17:09,373
Nine justices.

481
00:17:09,629 --> 00:17:10,570
Not one dissent.

482
00:17:11,192 --> 00:17:13,591
The taxpayer must render unto Caesar what

483
00:17:13,592 --> 00:17:15,063
is Caesar's, but no more.

484
00:17:15,352 --> 00:17:16,411
Her home was gone.

485
00:17:17,133 --> 00:17:19,372
But the rule that took it was gone too —

486
00:17:19,373 --> 00:17:21,233
and in at least a dozen states,

487
00:17:21,513 --> 00:17:22,866
a warning still stands.

488
00:17:25,438 --> 00:17:28,320
Geraldine Tyler never moved back into that condo.

489
00:17:29,289 --> 00:17:31,228
The Court could restore the principle,

490
00:17:31,229 --> 00:17:33,641
but not the ten years, or the front door,

491
00:17:33,729 --> 00:17:36,553
or the mornings that came in through her window.

492
00:17:36,669 --> 00:17:38,610
What it did restore was a limit —

493
00:17:38,969 --> 00:17:40,675
one the country had let slip.

494
00:17:41,263 --> 00:17:42,502
Surplus is only one way

495
00:17:42,503 --> 00:17:44,002
the state can take what is yours,

496
00:17:44,003 --> 00:17:45,415
and it is the narrowest.

497
00:17:46,089 --> 00:17:48,628
Next, we follow the money that never went to court

498
00:17:48,629 --> 00:17:49,429
at all:

499
00:17:49,809 --> 00:17:51,985
you owed no one a cent, broke no law,

500
00:17:52,209 --> 00:17:54,928
and they still emptied your bag at the airport —

501
00:17:54,929 --> 00:17:56,949
cash you earned, gone in a jetway,

502
00:17:57,974 --> 00:17:59,253
with no charge ever filed,

503
00:17:59,254 --> 00:18:01,489
and a long road to get any of it back.

504
00:18:02,243 --> 00:18:02,782
Subscribe,

505
00:18:02,783 --> 00:18:05,607
and watch what a single routine flight can cost.
